  "how": "Recorded on Windows 11 from Docker Desktop 4.85.0 (engine 29.6.2, its docker desktop command v0.4.3): running on 2026-10-11 (the answer of `docker container inspect` was added on 2026-10-12), then through a stop and a start on 2026-10-13. `docker desktop stop --detach` answered after 13 seconds. Stopped, `docker desktop status` never answered (it was still waiting after 16 seconds, every time it was asked), while `docker version` and `docker ps` failed at once (\"failed to connect to the docker API at npipe:////./pipe/dockerDesktopLinuxEngine; check if the path is correct and if the daemon is running\"). `docker desktop start --detach`, and `docker desktop start --timeout 300` too, then answered that Docker Desktop was already running and started nothing; its own program (`Docker Desktop.exe`, beside the folder of its command) started it, and its engine answered 8 seconds later. Docker Desktop was installed for one user; its command's place is given as Docker Desktop installs it for every user, which has the same folders. The containers' ids, names, images and labels, and the session id, are replaced by made-up ones of the same form. An answer's third value, when there is one, is how many seconds it took; an exit status of null means no answer came. On 2026-10-26 the `docker ps` template gained the labels clank.ports and clank.proxy (design 0024 §2): the command's key and each container's line were changed by hand, the two labels empty (\"\"), as Docker answers for a label a container lacks. On 2026-10-26 the template gained each container's networks (`.Networks`, design 0004 §3: the edge on every workspace's network): the command's key and each container's line were changed by hand, the networks made-up names joined with commas, as Docker answers them (a stopped container keeps its own).",
